<div class="bg-zinc-950 text-zinc-100">
  <.marketing_nav current_user={@current_user} current={:docs} />

  <section class="py-12 sm:py-16">
    <.docs_layout
      current="integrations-google-workspace"
      toc={[
        {"sso", "Single sign-on"},
        {"scim", "Directory sync"}
      ]}
    >
      <.docs_header current="integrations-google-workspace">
        <:dek>
          Connect Google Workspace to emisar: an Internal OAuth client and a locked, prefilled
          issuer. Google has no outbound SCIM — the directory-sync section covers the gap.
        </:dek>
      </.docs_header>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        The shortest setup of the five — there is no issuer to look up. You need a Google Cloud
        project in the same organization as your Workspace, and an emisar owner or admin.
      </p>

      <.docs_h2 id="sso">Single sign-on</.docs_h2>
      <ol class="mt-8 space-y-10">
        <li class="flex gap-4">
          <span class="grid h-8 w-8 flex-none place-items-center rounded-full bg-brand-500/20 text-sm font-semibold text-brand-300">
            1
          </span>
          <div class="min-w-0 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
            <.docs_step_venue>Google Cloud</.docs_step_venue>
            <p class="font-semibold text-zinc-100">Set the OAuth consent screen to Internal.</p>
            <p class="mt-1.5">
              In <strong class="text-zinc-200">Google Cloud Console → APIs &amp; Services → OAuth consent screen</strong>, choose <strong class="text-zinc-200">Internal</strong>. That restricts sign-in to your own Workspace domain and
              skips Google's verification review entirely. External would expose the app to any
              Google account, which is not what you want in front of emisar.
            </p>
          </div>
        </li>
        <li class="flex gap-4">
          <span class="grid h-8 w-8 flex-none place-items-center rounded-full bg-brand-500/20 text-sm font-semibold text-brand-300">
            2
          </span>
          <div class="min-w-0 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
            <.docs_step_venue>Google Cloud</.docs_step_venue>
            <p class="font-semibold text-zinc-100">Create the OAuth client.</p>
            <p class="mt-1.5">
              <strong class="text-zinc-200">Credentials → Create credentials → OAuth client ID</strong>, application type <strong class="text-zinc-200">Web application</strong>. Add emisar's callback under <strong class="text-zinc-200">Authorized redirect URIs</strong>:
            </p>
            <.docs_code
              phx-no-format
              label="Authorized redirect URI"
              copy_text="https://emisar.dev/sign_in/sso/callback"
            >https://emisar.dev/sign_in/sso/callback</.docs_code>
            <p class="mt-4">
              Google matches this string exactly — a trailing slash or
              <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">http</code>
              instead of <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">https</code>
              is rejected at sign-in with a redirect_uri_mismatch, not at save time.
            </p>
          </div>
        </li>
        <li class="flex gap-4">
          <span class="grid h-8 w-8 flex-none place-items-center rounded-full bg-brand-500/20 text-sm font-semibold text-brand-300">
            3
          </span>
          <div class="min-w-0 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
            <.docs_step_venue>emisar</.docs_step_venue>
            <p class="font-semibold text-zinc-100">Paste the credentials into emisar.</p>
            <p class="mt-1.5">
              Pick <strong class="text-zinc-200">Google Workspace</strong>
              as the provider type and paste the client ID and secret. The
              <strong class="text-zinc-200">Issuer URL</strong>
              is already filled in and locked —
              <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">https://accounts.google.com</code>
              for every Google customer — so it is one less thing to get wrong. Leave the identifier
              claim on <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">sub</code>.
            </p>

            <.docs_screenshot
              src="/images/docs/sso/google-emisar-connection.webp"
              title="emisar console · Add provider"
              caption="Picking Google Workspace locks the issuer to https://accounts.google.com — the padlock means there is nothing for you to look up or mistype."
              alt="emisar's Add provider form with Provider type set to Google Workspace, the in-app setup steps naming the Google Cloud Console path, the redirect URI to register with a copy button, and a locked Issuer URL field showing https://accounts.google.com."
            />
          </div>
        </li>
      </ol>

      <.docs_h2 id="scim">Directory sync</.docs_h2>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        Google Workspace cannot drive directory sync into emisar. Google provisions to SaaS apps in
        its own catalog, and a custom OAuth client — which is what
        the setup above creates — is not one of them, so there is no SCIM connector to point here. Members are
        created on first sign-in at the connection's default role, and nothing removes them
        automatically: suspending the Google account stops them signing in, which is the control
        that matters, then remove the member in <strong class="text-zinc-200">Team</strong>
        to close out their API keys. emisar's connection page hides the sync panel for this
        provider rather than offer a feature that could never connect.
      </p>
      <.docs_callout kind={:warn} title="Using Google Workspace?">
        <p>
          Google Workspace is a first-class OIDC <em>login</em>
          provider here, but it has no outbound SCIM — it won't push the user lifecycle to
          emisar. On its own you get just-in-time provisioning on first login and <strong class="text-zinc-200">no automatic deprovisioning</strong>. To close that gap,
          route Google through your IdP (Okta, Entra, or JumpCloud) and connect <em>that</em>
          to emisar's SCIM — an offboard in Google then reaches emisar as the standard
          <code class="rounded bg-zinc-950 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">active: false</code>
          signal and access is revoked automatically, exactly as above. Most orgs already
          federate Google through an IdP, so there's usually nothing new to stand up. (Native
          Google directory sync without an IdP is on our roadmap — talk to us if you need it.)
        </p>
      </.docs_callout>
    </.docs_layout>
  </section>

  <.marketing_footer />
</div>
